# syntax=codingbooth/boothfile:1

# Test: Boothfile `install code-extension` — arbitrary VS Code / code-server
# extension by Open VSX id.
#
# `install code-extension` needs an editor to install into, and the base variant
# ships none, so pull both in. Installing BOTH is the point: an extension must land
# in whichever editor is present, and the two live in different extension dirs with
# different CLIs. Real booths get one or the other from their variant — code-server
# from the codeserver variant, desktop VS Code from all four desktop variants — and
# neither of those paths selects a template, so only a base build can exercise both
# at once. On a real variant these two lines are unnecessary.
# `setup vscode` installs Jupyter + a Bash kernel with pip, and the base image has
# no python at all. The desktop variants never hit this because xfce--setup.sh runs
# python--setup.sh itself before VS Code; a bare base build has to ask.
setup python

setup codeserver
setup vscode

# `bmewburn.vscode-intelephense-client` would need PHP; pick something small and
# dependency-free so the test measures the install path, not the extension.
install code-extension mads-hartmann.bash-ide-vscode

# Rides along on the same build: the curated path, pinning the id that was wrong
# (JakeBecker.elixir-ls is the Microsoft Marketplace id and 404s on Open VSX).
# `install_extensions` warns and carries on when an id fails, so only an assertion
# on the installed list catches a regression here.
setup elixir-code-extension
